'''winter<sup>1</sup>''' (win′tėr), ''v.'' [< ME. ''wynteren'', ''wyntren'' = D. ''winteren'', be or become winter; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To spend or pass the winter; take winter quarters; hiemate; hibernate.
{{smaller block|And whan the hauene was not able for to dwelle in wynter, ful manye ordeyneden counseil for to . . . ''wynterne'' in the hauene of Crete.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Acts xxvii. 12.}}
After many dreadfull combates with the ice, and one of the shippes departing from the other, they were forced to ''winter'' in Noua Zemla.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 434.}}
I went to London with my family to ''winter'' at Soho, in the great square.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Nov. 27, 1689.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To overtake with winter; detain during winter. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|They sayled to the 49. degree and a halfe vnder the pole Antartyke; where beinge ''wyntered'', they were inforced to remayne there for the space of two monethes.
{{right|''R. Eden'', tr. of Antonio Pigafetta (First Books on America, ed. Arber, p. 251).}}}}
